            {
              "id": "glog-34f94acf-5451-44b3-82de-7b5c03952e7b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value for randomness seeding can lead to predictable randomness, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers generated by the program, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `srand(1)`, a static value of 1 is used to seed the random number generator. This means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated, making it easy for an attacker to predict.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. This could be a value that changes each time the program is run, such as the current time, or a value that is unpredictable, such as a random number generated by a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the current time to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nsrand(time(0));\n```\n\nThis code will generate a different sequence of random numbers each time the program is run, as long as the program is not run more than once per second. For more secure randomness, consider using a hardware random number generator or a cryptographic random number generator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- ctime\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
