            {
              "id": "glog-e5288f5b-d530-4a39-99c9-408399634710",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the source buffer is larger than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet `memcpy(dst, src, sizeof(*dst))`, the size of the destination buffer `dst` is used as the third argument to `memcpy`. If the size of the source buffer `src` is larger than `dst`, this can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause crashes, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the size of the destination buffer is at least as large as the source buffer. This can be done by checking the sizes of the buffers before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy` such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` which include additional safeguards against buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code that checks the sizes of the buffers before calling `memcpy`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\nvoid safe_memcpy(void* dst, size_t dst_size, const void* src, size_t src_size) {\n    if (src_size <= dst_size) {\n        memcpy(dst, src, src_size);\n    } else {\n        // Handle error: source buffer is larger than destination buffer\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is `<cstring>`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
